“Registration,” said Sofía. “That’s why the app. Every person who
walks in through those doors has downloaded the loop. And every
phone in the borough that has it installed will play the same thing at
the same second.” She put one finger on the line. “It’s a synchroniza‐
tion. A whole borough, on one cue, in one tone, for eleven minutes.”
   “To do what?” said Manny.
   “To set it.” I heard my own voice from far away. “A baseline.
Whatever the whole city feels in those eleven minutes is what they’ll
measure against. It’s a calibration.”
   Rafa lowered himself into a dining chair. Slowly, a man whose knees
had been folded for sixty-some years.
   “Nine-forty,” he said. “Eleven minutes.”
   Sofía took a pushpin and pinned the run-of-show to the wall at the
end of the red string. The wall had been a history. Now it had a date
and a time.




